#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
roundtable — put one question to a panel of independent models, in parallel.

Each lane is a different model, answering the same brief without seeing the
others. The product is *disagreement*: where independently-trained models land
in the same place, that convergence means something; where they split, that is
where you look.

Design notes worth reading before changing anything:

  * There are no long-lived processes. A lane is one HTTP request or one
    short-lived subprocess. An abandoned run cannot keep billing, because there
    is nothing left running to bill.
  * There is no delivery step. Answers come back in the response or the lane
    failed. Nothing can "succeed" into a place nobody reads.
  * `harness`, not `model`, decides cost. See CLI lanes below.

Usage:
    roundtable "the brief"
    cat brief.md | roundtable -
    roundtable --lanes Auditor,Builder "the brief"
    roundtable --list
    roundtable --list --json               roster as JSON for tools (no secret refs)
    roundtable --json > answers.json
    roundtable --each --run-id ID "..."    tag every transcript of this run with ID
    roundtable --synthesize latest:12      --diff readers over a finished run's
                                           transcripts; no lane is re-asked
    roundtable --config PATH
    roundtable --max-spend 0.50
    cat brief.md | roundtable --each -j 8 -  eight lanes at a time, each still
                                           its own process and its own deadline
    roundtable --each --revise latest:12   optional second round: each lane sees
                                           the locked round-1 answers and may
                                           HOLD or REVISE
    roundtable --discuss latest:12         shared-context discussion seeded from
                                           that round: lanes take turns in ONE
                                           thread, one lane in flight at a time,
                                           then each closes with HOLD or REVISE
    roundtable --discuss latest:12 --discuss-rounds 3 "focus on the cost claims"
    cat brief.md | roundtable --discuss new -   start cold, no blind round
    roundtable --converse latest:12        the same flag, by its other name

Store (#105; runs do not read it yet -- YAML stays authoritative until #109):
    roundtable db init | migrate | doctor  [--config DB]
    roundtable secrets set NAME [--stdin]  value from stdin or a no-echo prompt,
                                           never argv
    roundtable secrets check [NAME...] | list | rotate NAME | rm NAME [--force]
    roundtable secrets rekey

  * Round 1 is blind by construction — a lane cannot see the others because
    their answers do not exist yet in its process. `--revise` and `--discuss`
    are the only paths to cross-lane visibility: both replay a *finished* run's
    transcript(s), so the openings each lane receives are locked before any
    lane reads them. Answers after either are not independent and the report
    says so; convergence there is persuasion, not evidence.
"""
